WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.760
 So now we're going to take a look at how we deal with

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.680
 more than one table, multiple tables.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.040
 'Cause the real power of SQL and the power of database

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.160
 performance has to do with when you start connecting

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:11.000
 tables together.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.760
 If you go back to that original mathematics,

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.320
 it models data at the intersections between the row

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:16.300
 and the columns.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.180
 And these intersections are the magical bits.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:24.040
 And so, breaking an application to use multiple tables

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:25.080
 is an art form.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.760
 It takes a while.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.640
 There are some simple basic things that you can learn

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:30.920
 and will teach you here.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.200
 And so it's not too hard to learn the basics,

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:37.880
 but then it's much more complex to be super skilled at it.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:40.760
 And in general, advanced databases, in my mind,

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:43.840
 it's hard to teach advanced databases,

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:47.680
 because they're always so contextually grounded.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:51.560
 You know, something like Twitter or Google,

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:53.680
 the databases are so specialized.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:55.120
 By the time you make,

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.240
 everyone can do small to medium sized databases

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:58.600
 using the basic techniques,

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.820
 but at some point, once you escape medium sized databases,

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.600
 you end up in these sort of narrow things

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.080
 and optimize each database very separately.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.160
 And so I just tell people, you know,

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.320
 learn the basics really, really well,

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.360
 write programs and then go do real work.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:18.280
 But database design is the act of figuring out

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:23.220
 the data that your application is going to want to store

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.260
 and spreading that across multiple tables.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:26.480
 But we don't just do it randomly.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:28.680
 We do it very much cleverly.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.680
 And if you look at a data model,

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:31.720
 this is what it looks like.

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:34.360
 And what we're showing here in this data model

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:38.440
 is we are showing five tables,

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.240
 and this is a kind of a calendar kind of a system,

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:44.440
 and we're seeing the columns that are in each of the tables,

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:47.680
 and then we're seeing the relationships between the tables.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:49.160
 And even in these relationships,

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:51.000
 there's kind of a little bit of code.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.960
 And when you have an arrow that looks like that,

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:54.300
 there's many of those to one,

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.800
 and this is a many-to-one relationship,

00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:58.760
 many-to-one relationship.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:00.640
 We'll talk all about that stuff.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:02.280
 But if you go into an organization

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.480
 and you have a really large and complex data application,

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:07.040
 they might have something printed out on the wall

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:08.380
 that looks about like this,

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:11.320
 which shows the database tables and connections,

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:12.320
 et cetera, et cetera.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:14.380
 And they might say, oh, your job is to go down

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.480
 and then this little corner, add one column,

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:18.320
 field there, and then do this,

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:19.840
 and then connect it with this thing over there,

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.300
 and then make a screen that shows all these things

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.080
 that pulls from this table, this table,

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:25.480
 this table, and that table.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:27.960
 And that's your job, if you're a programmer

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.860
 on a large software development project.

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:33.420
 These database models become sort of like

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.560
 the core backbone of the knowledge

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:40.120
 that applications are managing and using.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:43.120
 So, the idea is is that you take your application,

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:44.180
 we're going to start really simple,

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:45.560
 we're going to take your application,

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.160
 and you have to draw a picture.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.880
 And the basic rule, and literally,

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:52.200
 you could spend course upon course

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:54.560
 learning about database normalization.

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:57.240
 But I'm going to distill it into one basic rule,

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:01.520
 and that is never put the same string data in twice.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:04.400
 So, my name, Charles Severance,

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.180
 if I build a database well,

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:07.440
 you should go into that database,

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.600
 and you'd say, okay, the words Charles Severance,

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.720
 which is the name of a person, me, in that database,

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.280
 only shows up once.

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:17.300
 And what we do instead is we connect things together

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:20.100
 and model my name as a connection to the record

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:21.860
 that has my actual name in it,

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:24.000
 rather than putting my name all these other places.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.000
 And so, the idea is to pull duplicate data out

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.520
 and make only one copy of it.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:32.280
 So, there is the users, and in there is the user's name,

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.840
 and the user name shows up only here,

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:38.280
 and everything else points to the particular user entry.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:40.360
 So, that's the idea.

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:43.940
 And so, here is our first application.

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:46.460
 We are working as a startup.

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:48.080
 We just quit all of our jobs,

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:51.560
 and we are going to build a music management application.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:52.720
 I mean, what a great idea,

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:54.920
 don't you think that'll be quite successful?

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:57.540
 And so, we have mocked up and we have figured out

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:00.040
 that this is what our music management application,

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:03.280
 we want to track people's tracks,

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:05.320
 know something about what artists and albums

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:07.320
 and genre they are, and we want to have ratings,

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:09.640
 and how many times we played them, and how long they are.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:13.000
 That's the data that our application needs to represent.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.800
 And we've done testing on this and wireframes,

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:18.440
 and everyone loves this, a great user interface.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:20.900
 And so, this is how it's got to look.

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:23.640
 But we're going to have billions and billions of tracks

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:24.960
 in these things, and so we want to come up

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:27.960
 with an efficient database to handle this.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:29.600
 And so, we're going to take a look at this,

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:31.120
 and look at each of the columns,

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:32.860
 and we're going to ask ourselves,

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:37.520
 is this column part of one of our existing objects,

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:41.520
 our existing tables, or has this object

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.180
 have to create a new table?

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:45.500
 And then, once we find those different objects,

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:48.360
 we connect the tables together and model the connections.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:51.360
 Now, a little trick to kind of make it a little easier

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:54.080
 on ourselves is we can look in these columns,

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:56.600
 and look in the columns that have duplicate information

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:58.560
 vertically that's string information.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:01.500
 So, a rating is just a number like zero through five.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.360
 So, we don't worry too much about integers,

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:06.120
 and numbers, and that kind of stuff, or whatever,

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:07.360
 but we do look for strings.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:08.800
 And the problem here is we got like,

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:11.080
 these strings occur many times.

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:12.520
 And so, these are the problems.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:14.800
 And so, we have to put these things

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.200
 where there is replication of string data

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:18.760
 kind of in the vertical dimension.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:22.160
 We have to put those in different tables.

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:23.880
 And so, we'll start out.

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:26.280
 Now, the first question that you have to ask yourself

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:27.440
 when you're going to draw this picture

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.300
 of how this data is in multiple tables

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:32.160
 and connect it together, is what is the first one

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:33.800
 that you're going to write down?

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:35.360
 And this is an interesting debate,

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:36.880
 and often people are sitting in a conference room,

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:39.720
 and people who have experience kind of know what to do.

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:42.600
 Usually, if it's a multi-user system,

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.640
 like a learning management system,

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:46.760
 the users might be the central concept,

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:49.280
 perhaps the courses might be the central concept.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:52.080
 This is a single-user system, and so you can think,

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.720
 well, what is really this application about?

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:57.360
 It's not about people, it's one person.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:59.960
 But it is about tracks.

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:04.900
 And so, we can say, okay, here we'll take the track

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:08.160
 is probably the sort of most foundational notion

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:09.720
 of this application.

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:11.600
 And then we can take and say, okay,

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:13.920
 now that we've decided that tracks

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:15.760
 are the foundational notion,

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:19.960
 which of these columns are simply an attribute of the track?

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:23.840
 Not really the cheating way and the easy way,

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:26.340
 and this particular one is like these numbers,

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:29.080
 all these numbers, like this number and these numbers,

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:31.280
 not that one. (laughs)

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:33.660
 They just go along with track, and so we'll put that in.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:36.460
 We got the track title, rating, length, and count,

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:38.060
 and we put that in.

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:42.560
 And then the question is, we've got the remaining things,

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:44.720
 we've got the artist, we've got the album,

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:46.200
 and we've got the genre.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.960
 And so, we can say, okay, well, we can't,

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:50.700
 we've got some vertical duplication,

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:53.480
 so we're going to say, okay, this track probably

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:56.600
 belongs to an album, so let's pull out the album

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:58.100
 into its own table.

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:00.600
 Oops.

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:05.080
 Pull the album out into its own table.

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:05.900
 And so,

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:18.420
 pull the album out into its own table.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:20.720
 And so, that pulls that out, and then you say, okay,

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:22.860
 what would be the next thing that we're going to pull out?

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:25.720
 So, we pulled out the track, we've got this taken care of,

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:28.900
 this taken care of, that taken, now we've got the album.

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:31.380
 Well, albums belong to artists, so let's take out

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:35.560
 the artist, and then we'll pick where the genre belongs,

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:38.080
 and we'll just say that the genre belongs to the track,

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:40.780
 and so, because there might be albums with more than

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:43.780
 one different genre, so it either belongs to the album,

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:45.920
 or a different genre, so each album is not necessarily

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:47.660
 a rock album, it could have a rock track,

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:50.240
 and a country track, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:53.020
 And so, now what we've got is we've got four tables, right?

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:54.840
 We've got a track table, we've got an album table,

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:57.360
 an artist table, and a genre table, and if we sort of

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:59.500
 double check, all of the columns that had vertical

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:03.660
 duplication in them now have their own little table.

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:07.800
 So, we can eliminate, the next thing we'll do is to show

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:11.960
 how we're going to eliminate this vertical data replication

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:16.900
 by showing how you represent these relationships

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:20.820
 that we just created inside of the database.

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:23.820
 (heavy metal music)

